    description: "**Proposal**: add a container-membership validator class to the ADR-0033 spec-vs-data validator stage — \"for each (language, container-kind) pair emitted in this run, at least one container of that pair roots at least one member\" — as the fixture-free complement to the G2 emission-parity matrix's `emits_enum_members` / `emits_abstract_members` columns.\n\n**Why the validator rather than more fixtures.** WI-duguk drained those columns 8 holes to 0 by hand-authoring one uniform-construct fixture per language. That is a per-site sweep: it scales at one artifact per language, and the correctness strategy's through-line says the answer to \"N producers, no shared contract\" is a chokepoint plus a content-gated validator, never a sweep. The validator stage already IS that reader — ADR-0033 exists because \"each pass is a writer; no pass is a reader\" — so this property belongs there rather than in a twelfth, twentieth and seventieth fixture.\n\n**What it catches that the matrix structurally cannot.** ADR-0033 places the validator after ALL enrichment passes, so it sees `contains` edges, not just symbols. WI-duguk needed TWO separate fixes for one user-visible symptom: swift.py not emitting protocol requirements (analyzer side) and `protocol` missing from `linkers.containment.CONTAINER_KINDS` + the slicer's set (pipeline side). The emission-parity column measures ONE ANALYZER IN ISOLATION via span nesting and could only ever see the first; swift read green on that column while the live reverse slice was unmoved at 2 nodes. A post-linker check sees both failure modes as one signal, because both produce the same observable: a container with zero contains-out.\n\n**Why the WI-rubip ambiguity does not apply here** (this is the load-bearing claim; the matrix went to injected fixtures precisely to escape it). In general an empty cell is ambiguous — analyzer gap, or construct absent from the substrate? For container membership it is not: if an `enum` NODE exists in the output, the enum construct was present in the source by definition. The container's own presence is the applicability proof. That is what makes this one property checkable corpus-derived across every analyzer with no fixture at all.\n\n**Where the corpus-absence hazard moves to instead.** It does not vanish, it relocates: a (language, container-kind) pair that never appears in the run is UNEXERCISED, and must be reported as such rather than folded into \"clean\". Corpus-absence is not non-existence. The report needs three states per pair — exercised-and-rooted, exercised-and-zero, not-exercised — and only the second is a violation.\n\n**Design decisions the implementation must make, none of them settled here:**\n1. Aggregation unit and threshold. `enum Empty {}` is legal, so a single zero-member container proves nothing; the signal is a (language, kind) pair where N containers exist and ZERO of them root anything. What N is, and whether the rule is 0% or a low percentage, is a calibration question that wants a measurement on real repos before a number is picked.\n2. Severity. Should be `warning`, not `error`: a legitimately memberless container exists, and ADR-0033's realized gate is a shrink-only per-substrate ratchet against committed baselines rather than assert-empty. This joins that ratchet; it does not get to be a hard gate on day one.\n3. `validator_class` is a bounded-enum on `ValidationViolation`. Adding a value is an axis change under ADR-0024 and needs its scaffolding, or a justified reuse of an existing class.\n4. Whether the per-pair census belongs in `validation_report` as structured data (so a consumer can read \"this language's enums were never rooted\") or only as violation records.\n\n**Honest limits — state these before anyone treats it as fleet-wide coverage:**\n- **Kotlin defeats it, and defeats the matrix too.** `enum class Color` is emitted as `kind=\"class\"`, so its zero-member enums hide among healthy classes and no kind-keyed aggregate will separate them. Neither instrument can see this. That is an argument for fixing the kind emission, not for building a cleverer detector around it.\n- **Partial gaps are diluted.** A (language, kind) pair where some containers root and others do not will read as healthy. This catches the 0% class — which is, empirically, every gap WI-duguk found — and not the long tail.\n- **The CI breadth win is smaller than it looks.** The validator gate's substrate is `tests/fixtures/schema-coverage-corpus/` (bash, csharp, go, java, rust, solidity, sql, swift, typescript, wgsl — 10 languages). That is not dramatically broader than the matrix's 7 gated analyzers, and it is a different 10. Do not sell this as \"70 languages in CI\"; in CI it is ten, plus whatever the corpus grows.\n- **The real breadth win is distribution, not CI.** A validator check ships inside the tool and runs on the USER's repo. Someone pointing hypergumbo at a Scala or Kotlin codebase gets the warning in their own `validation_report`, for languages this project will never write a fixture for. For a tool whose value proposition is breadth across ~70 languages while its quality guarantee covers 7, that is the gap this closes — and it closes it by making the artifact disclose its own blind spot rather than by promising coverage that does not exist.\n\n**Explicit non-goal: this does not replace the G2 matrix.** The matrix locks eleven per-field emission properties (signature, qualified_name, is_exported, docstring, complexity, edge_calls, edge_imports, shape_id, emits_variable, emits_field, and the two container-member columns) with a strict-xfail ratchet. This checks ONE structural property. They are complementary: the matrix is deep on a few languages, this is shallow on all of them. Removing either loses real coverage.\n\n**Related**: WI-duguk (closed — the drain that produced this finding); WI-dorop (scala/cpp/kotlin container-member defects, which this check would surface automatically once those languages are in a corpus); ADR-0033 (the validator stage this extends); ADR-0024 (axis governance for a new `validator_class` value); the G2 matrix's declared-scope maps in `test_emission_parity_matrix.py`, which name the ungated languages this is meant to reach."  # 9093
